    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 011136\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n436 AM MST Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n.UPDATE...12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry conditions with zero rain chances will persist through at\n  least the weekend and likely much of next week.\n\n- As high pressure builds over the region, a gradual warming trend\n  will bring temperatures at least into a normal range starting\n  this weekend and eventually slightly above normal for next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nA much quieter weather pattern is taking shape across much of the\nWestern U.S. as an unseasonably strong high pressure ridge near \nthe West Coast moves inland through Friday. The center of the\nridge is forecast to set up over the Desert Southwest by Saturday\nand likely stay there well into next week. In the short term, \nsome of the 'cooler' air remains over Arizona, but with abundant \nsunshine and increasing heights aloft daytime highs will be back \nin the lower 90s across the Phoenix area to as warm as the upper \n90s across southeast California. Once the center of the ridge \nmoves into our region on Friday, additional warming can be \nexpected bringing lower desert highs up to around 95 degrees in \nthe Phoenix area to around 100 degrees across southeast California\nand the Lower CO River Valley. Despite the drier air aloft, \nsurface moisture will stay a bit elevated as breezy easterly \nwinds are expected to set up beginning Friday morning. This \neasterly fetch, with morning gusts easily reaching 20-25 mph \nacross southern Arizona will supply the area with some weak \nmoisture advection in the low levels. This moisture will not be \nnearly enough to bring any rain chances, but it will keep surface \ndew points in the 50s. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nLittle will change over the weekend and likely even for the bulk\nof next week. The high pressure ridge is heavily favored to remain\nover or at least very close to our region well into next week\nkeeping us under dry air aloft but with a persistent easterly low\nlevel semi-moist flow. Subsidence under the ridge and the dry air\nshould keep a lid on any potential convective chances most days.\nThere are some hints at maybe some convective potential early next\nweek over the highest peaks in eastern Arizona, but as of now the\nhighest PoPs barely breach 10% over the White Mountains.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to rise a bit further during the first\nhalf of next week with the western deserts likely reaching 100-104\ndegrees, while the south-central Arizona lower deserts may get\nclose to 100 degrees. These early October temperatures would range\nfrom 4-8 degrees above normal. Overnight lows will also be at\nleast a few degrees above normal, but generally staying in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s. The most interesting part of the forecast\nwill be the daily easterly breezy conditions due to the expected\nposition of the high over or just to our north and a weak trough\nsetting up to our south. The easterly winds should reach their\npeak over the weekend and during the mid to late morning hours \nwith gusts in some places reaching 30-35 mph. As daytime heating \nincreases and the mixed layer rises, the winds will be more \ndispersed and decrease during the afternoon hours. We should keep \nthese daily easterly mostly morning breezes through at early next\nweek and possibly longer. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1135Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo aviation concerns under clear skies can be anticipated through\nthe forecast period. There is still a high degree of uncertainty\nin wind directions this afternoon with some models indicating a\nwesterly shift whereas others indicate winds maintaining an\neasterly direction. Overall an E-SE component is slightly favored\nand speeds should remain very light, with extended periods of \nvariability likely through this afternoon.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather issues will exist through the forecast period\nunder clear skies. Winds will be predominantly SE at KIPL and N-NE\nat KBLH with speeds remaining aob 6 kts. Extended stretches of \ncalm and vrb winds will also be common at both terminals.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nGradual drying as high pressure takes hold over the region will\nlead to somewhat lower humidities and mostly clear skies through \nthe rest of the week. Near normal temperatures are expected with \nthe warmest temperatures consistently across the western \ndistricts. MinRHs will mostly range from 15-25% over the western \ndistricts to 25-35% for the eastern districts with good to \nexcellent overnight recoveries. After light winds today, breezy \neast winds will set up tonight into Friday and persist for several\ndays. Morning wind gusts of 20-30 mph will be common starting \nFriday. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Salerno\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
